# Observability Strategy

Defines logging, diagnostics, analytics readiness, and privacy constraints.

## Mandatory rules

- Logs must be structured enough to diagnose issues.
- Must not log secrets, tokens, passwords, sensitive payloads, or private user data.
- Must use environment-based log levels and disable verbose logs in production builds.
- Must capture startup, auth, API, sync, and storage failures with safe context.
- Analytics should stay optional and app-specific; must not force a vendor into the base template.
- Breadcrumbs or context may be added only when they must not expose sensitive information.

## Log levels

| Level | Use |
|---|---|
| debug | development-only details |
| info | normal lifecycle events |
| warning | recoverable unexpected state |
| error | failed operation requiring attention |

## Acceptance checklist

- Production logs are safe.
- Failure diagnostics help reproduce issues without exposing private data.
- Logging can be disabled or adjusted per environment.

